this morning the world's largest music  streaming site is going public on the  New York Stock Exchange analysts say the  company could be valued at more than  twenty billion dollars that's with the  be Spotify will launch with the direct  listing of shares not an IPO so this  removes the traditional role of  investment banks  it means existing Spotify shareholders  can sell stocks directly to the public  while the company says the goal is to  make investment in the business more  transparent so only on CBS this morning  Spotify co-founder and CEO Daniel act  joins us at the table to discuss  congratulations is in order to you mr.  act this morning thank you but clearly  you didn't get the memo because normally  people do this they're down on the stock  floor they're ready to bail they're  taking picture right everybody's  congratulating him you're here because  you wanted to do it differently why well  I I really think that when you look at  our company and when I started thinking  about this we started this company when  I was 23 years old what were you  thinking at the time well I mean for me  someone growing up in suburbs  working-class suburb in Stockholm I  couldn't afford all the music so back in  98 99 I was really thinking about how I  could get all that music and do it in a  legal way while at the same time  compensating the artists and that's  where the idea of Spotify came up and so  we're now like a decade into that  journey and I really just feel like  we're in the second inning so so while  this is obviously a big day and I'm very  proud of my employees I really just feel  like we're in the early days not  celebrating the end days as so many  other companies like you are doing what  have you learned about the way people  listen to music one thing obviously is  they're willing to stream music instead  of having it in their heads but what  else are you learning about the way  people have behaved in this new world  well I think the most interesting things  is when you have all the world's music  in your pocket you start listening to a  lot more music than you ever did before  by a lot more artists than you ever did  before  and in very different moments of time so  take New Yorkers as an example it turns  out that the number one  New Yorkers listen to music is between  10:00 p.m. and 11:00 p.m. even a week  days whoa it's really the city that  never sleeps right yeah 10:00 p.m. I  made a playlist it took me an hour you  really can focus on what people are  listening to and when they're listening  to it yeah and we can get you on all  platforms that's the thing I was at the  dentist yesterday they said what do you  want to hear we have everything on  Spotify Bruce Springsteen's greatest  hits I mean so instantly they queue it  up yeah exactly and that's the  interesting thing and that's whether  you're in your car or whether you're in  your home or whether you're at the gym  and and that's the other thing that's  really different about how consumers are  doing this they're they're really going  for gym playlists they're going for the  barbeque playlists that we have in  really setting different moods and  moments later but Daniel you stepped on  some toes because the artists let's take  Tara for instance they thought they  weren't fairly compensated right so you  know you bruised a lot of people's  feelings and now you and Taylor are back  together there's a song she's got a song  love story right it says baby just say  yes is that what you did it was slightly  more complicated but look I mean I for  me first off like I should have done a  much better job communicating this so I  take full ownership for doing that go to  her yeah I went to Nashville many many  times to talk to her team spend more  time directly explaining the model why  screaming mattered and and the great  news is I think she saw how streaming  was growing I think she saw that fans  were asking for it so eventually when  the new album came up she came to  Stockholm and spent some time with our  team they're figuring out a way that  made sense for her you've got some  enviable rivals including Apple I see  you wearing an Apple watch so you  haven't banned the product selves but  the Wall Street Journal is reporting  that they're going to take over is the  number one streaming site by this summer  are you skir you what are you doing  you're scared well we are about twice  the size of them so I think we've still  got some room and  I'm very happy with the growth that  we're seeing in our business I can't  speak for them but I feel pretty calm  but I do a little concern though well I  the funny thing is like when you have  something like music that billions of  people around the world care about  you're never gonna be alone right yeah  like this is too big and it matters too  for too many people  so actually what we found is when we've  got competition it actually grows the  market because more people are now  talking about streaming it's easy to  forget that just three years ago even in  the u.s. streaming wasn't really a  saying it was still downloading songs so  this helps educate the market and that's  equally true across the world be honest  are you gonna follow the stock price at  all today well I'll look at it when it  opens but but my focus is really on the  long term yeah you're always looking at  the big picture you're not like the  other kids in the class sorry they call  you a slayer and a savior what do you  say well III don't know I I don't really  focus on that I focus on getting people  to listen to more music than ever before  I know surprised I'm here at all thanks  so much thank you so much for having me  congratulations and you can always  discover the music we play on our  broadcast by following the show Spotify  playlist check CBS this morning dot-com  for daily playlists  posts
 

